  "articleSummary": "The article, \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" argues that the agentic AI threat is not a single future breakthrough but two compounding curves: endurance (how long one agent can run autonomous tasks, per METR's time-horizon data) and breadth (how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate, illustrated by Anthropic's disclosed GTG-1002 espionage campaign using Claude Code). Its central claim is that attackers using stolen cloud credentials, such as LLMjacking as described by Sysdig, reroute the cost to victims, so price and access gates that filter buyers fail against tenants, and that repeated cheap attempts make even low per-attempt success rates likely to succeed (illustrated with the 1 − (1 − p)^n formula). The intended audience is security and engineering teams who dismiss agentic threats as hype, and the tone is a pointed, opinionated analytical essay with a sharp, Terminator-derived framing: \"absolutely will not stop\" recast as \"absolutely will not stop billing,\" with endurance as the core metaphor. Technical terms to keep consistent in translation include \"orchestrator,\" \"sub-agent,\" \"LLMjacking,\" \"time-horizon,\" \"endurance,\" \"breadth,\" and \"per-attempt failure rate,\" and the post uses figures and code blocks to support its quantitative reasoning.",
